WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.290
No matter where you get your textures for use inside

2
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.009
of Unreal Engine 5, whether it's something

3
00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:07.690
you've created yourself or something

4
00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.330
you've downloaded from online or purchased

5
00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.849
from one of the many online asset stores,

6
00:00:12.849 --> 00:00:15.890
you need to make sure that texture adheres to a few

7
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:16.850
rules.

8
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.250
The first rule is that no matter

9
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.969
what dimension that texture is in the x or the y-axis,

10
00:00:22.969 --> 00:00:26.089
that dimension needs to be a power of 2.

11
00:00:26.089 --> 00:00:29.109
That means that the x-axis or the y-axis

12
00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:36.509
needs to be something like 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512,

13
00:00:36.509 --> 00:00:42.390
1024, 2048, 4096, and 8192.

14
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:47.429
Anything from one of those numbers in the x or the y-axis

15
00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:50.880
will give you a texture that will

16
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.679
work with Unreal's built-in texture streaming systems,

17
00:00:55.679 --> 00:00:58.399
as well as make sure that that texture packs more

18
00:00:58.439 --> 00:01:00.240
efficiently into memory.

19
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.359
When you import a texture into Unreal,

20
00:01:02.359 --> 00:01:04.599
it's going to tell you a number of different things.

21
00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:08.680
For example, it'll first tell you the imported size,

22
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.359
as well as the streaming method, the size it takes on disk,

23
00:01:12.359 --> 00:01:14.319
and the number of mipmaps.

24
00:01:14.319 --> 00:01:18.319
Now, mipmap might be a new term to many people.

25
00:01:18.319 --> 00:01:21.200
And mipmap is something that Unreal

26
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.799
does behind the scenes if your texture meets

27
00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:25.840
this power of 2 requirement.

28
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.480
Mipmaps are level of details for your texture,

29
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.640
and they're a way to help reduce your texture's cost when

30
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.019
it's viewed in a distance very far away from the camera.

31
00:01:35.019 --> 00:01:38.079
We'll cover mipmapping in the next few slides.

32
00:01:38.079 --> 00:01:40.079
But it's important to understand

33
00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:42.120
that in order to have this advantage

34
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.200
and have Unreal do this work for you,

35
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.359
your texture needs to maintain that power of 2 ratio.

36
00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:51.359
Let's take a look at the next texture.

37
00:01:51.359 --> 00:01:54.959
This next texture, again, imported at 1024 by 1024.

38
00:01:54.959 --> 00:01:57.040
Note a method, virtual stream.

39
00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.719
That's right, if we're dealing with virtual textures,

40
00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:02.200
no matter if those are streaming virtual textures

41
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.159
or runtime virtual textures,

42
00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:07.930
we need to ensure that the data we're feeding into there

43
00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.469
meets this power of 2 requirement.

44
00:02:10.469 --> 00:02:13.110
As you can see, we still have a very small resource size

45
00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.870
because we're using virtual textures

46
00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.580
and very important number of mips, 11.

47
00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.500
The last version of this texture,

48
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.520
imported at 1,000 by 1,000, method not streamed.

49
00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.199
Resource size, 512, number of mips, 1.

50
00:02:27.199 --> 00:02:28.539
This is very bad.

51
00:02:28.539 --> 00:02:30.439
This means that this particular texture,

52
00:02:30.439 --> 00:02:31.759
no matter where we use it

53
00:02:31.759 --> 00:02:34.520
or no matter how far away it is from the camera,

54
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.879
will always be used at full resolution.

55
00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:39.439
And this is gonna create a number of different problems,

56
00:02:39.439 --> 00:02:42.860
both visually and for performance.

57
00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.099
Now, in order to check any of your texture settings,

58
00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:47.800
we can open up Unreal.

59
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.840
And by double clicking on a texture, like so,

60
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.180
we'll open it up inside of the texture editor.

61
00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.280
And in the details at the top is where all of the settings

62
00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.159
for this texture should be located.
